\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Counting-basis relations at the real specialization}
\date{2026-11-10}

\p{Fix the mass by #{m^2=1}, as in the
[arithmetic family](fdip-000U), and use its coordinate basis
#{\delta_0,\ldots,\delta_3}. At #{t=1}, put
#{q_1(x)=x^TQ(1)x} and
#{c_i=\iota_{q_1}(\delta_i)}. Then
##{c_i^2=3\,1,\qquad
c_ic_j+c_jc_i=2Q(1)_{ij}\,1.}
The base-change map #{\beta_{\mathbb R}} of
\ref{fdip-000V} identifies these relations with
#{\mathbb R\otimes_{R_q}C}. Direct evaluation and evaluation
through either localization give the same algebra and action.
This evaluation is not injective: it kills the nonzero element
#{t-1} of #{R_q}. The isomorphism requires no injectivity.
The [effective rank-two form](fdip-000W) has its own Clifford
algebra on #{X_{\mathrm{elim}}}; it is separate from this
rank-4 construction.}

\proof{
\p{In this basis #{s=t+2}, so the diagonal entry of #{Q(1)}
is #{3}. Thus #{q_1(\delta_i)=3} and #{c_i^2=3\,1}. The
unhalved anticommutator on these coordinate generators is
#{2Q(1)_{ij}\,1}. The ring map #{R_q\to\mathbb R} sending
#{t} to #{1} kills #{t-1}, and #{t-1} is nonzero in #{R_q}.}
}
